# Pentair IntelliFlo VS+SVRS for Home Assistant

A Home Assistant integration that runs on my Pentair IntelliFlo VS+SVRS pump over RS-485 bus. Quoting to google, it should also work on:

```

• IntelliFlo & IntelliPro series (IntelliFlo VSF, IntelliFlo3 with proper communication setup, etc.)
• WhisperFlo VST
• SuperFlo VST (manufactured after November 20, 2020)
• SuperMax VS (newer VST/VS iterations)
```
But not on pumps without RS-485, eg
```
• Early SuperFlo VS: Units made prior to November 20, 2020 feature a local digital display/keypad but no RS-485 port. (Newer versions like the SuperFlo VST added RS-485).
• Standard Single-Speed Pumps: Traditional single-speed models (e.g., WhisperFlo, SuperFlo, SuperMax, and Sta-Rite equivalents) run via basic mechanical or external timer switches rather than digital data buses.
• Pumps Lacking Automation Drives: Any Pentair variable-speed pump that has been retrofitted with a third-party or basic non-communicating drive un

```
I get to RS-485 through a generic TCP<>RS-485 gateway, so Home Assistant talks plain TCP and needs no serial hardware of its own.

The integration acts as the pump's controller. It sets power and speed and every so often reports what the pump is doing. Whilst in control the pump the panel on the pump is disabled. You get it back when the pump is turned off in the integration.

## Hardware

- A Pentair IntelliFlo VS+SVRS on its RS-485 terminals.
- TCP<>RS-485 gateway wired to the same pair, set to 9600 baud, 8N1

The gateway serves one client at a time. Port 4196.

## Installation

With [HACS](https://hacs.xyz): add this repository as a custom repository of type Integration, install it, and restart Home Assistant.

By hand: copy `custom_components/intelliflo` into your Home Assistant `config/custom_components/` directory and restart.

## Setup

Add the integration under Settings > Devices & services and give it the gateway's host and port. It brings its own power switch and speed slider. Turning the switch on or moving the slider sends the command, and the status sensor shows the pump ramping until it gets there.

On its first connect the integration takes over whatever the pump is already doing: a running pump keeps running at its current speed, and a stopped one stays off. After that the switch and slider are in charge.

Speed in percent maps linearly onto 1300-3449 rpm, and 0% means 0 rpm, so the pump stays stopped at 0% even with the power switch on.

In IntelliFlo.py INIT_REGISTERS is a list of registers and init values. If you change these values manually, they'll get reset.

These values work for me, but I'm not sure of the significance of prime delay, prime max time and prime sensitivity. The SVRS restart enable may not be on your pump, but shouldn't do anything other than give you an error in the log. You may not want to use it anyway, since it's supposed to stop pump damage from full baskets etc. I need it for piping reasons.

## Entities

| Entity         | Meaning                                                   |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| power (switch) | whether the pump should run                               |
| speed (number) | the speed it should run at, 0-100%                        |
| rpm            | measured speed                                            |
| w              | power draw, in W                                          |
| percent        | measured speed as a percentage of the 1300-3449 rpm range |
| status         | disconnected, off, running, ramping, stopping or stalled  |

The switch and slider hold what you asked for; the sensors show what the pump is doing. A command given while the pump cannot be reached is applied when it comes back.

The three readings go unavailable while the pump cannot be reached, and they only update on a change of more than 5% or every 20 seconds, which keeps the recorder quiet. The status sensor carries the raw status fields and the two SVRS alarm registers as attributes.

## Settings written to the pump

Each time the integration connects, it writes these registers, so changes made at the pump's own panel are put back. A running pump refuses the writes, so if it is running they wait until it next stops.

| Setting             | Value                         |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| ramp                | 100                           |
| max rpm             | 3449                          |
| min rpm             | 1300                          |
| prime enable        | 1                             |
| prime delay         | 1                             |
| prime max time      | 3                             |
| prime sensitivity   | 10                            |
| SVRS restart enable | 1, or 0 with SVRS restart off |
| SVRS restart timer  | 30 s                          |

They live in `INIT_REGISTERS` in `intelliflo.py`.

## Behaviour on restart

If you kill the integration or stop ha, the pump continues doing  whatever it was doing.  After
a while the pump will stop anyway.  Home Assistant restores the two helpers at startup, and the integration drives the pump back to them.

## Tests

```sh
pytest
```

